  "query_preview": "..Not expecting to see any major weather events this \ncoming week. However, as high pressure in the Gulf breaks down, \nseveral minor disturbances will move through the area, leading to \nthe development of showery precipitation. By Sunday, the lingering\nremnants of a front will support rain showers primarily in the \nnorthern panhandle. 24 hour rainfall amounts on Sunday are \nexpected to be around 0.1\u201d, with the highest rates occurring \novernight into Sunday morning. Most precipitation will be isolated\nto higher elevations.\n\nSomething of note, some models are indicating favorable lower level \nlapse rates along the international border, which may help \nthunderstorm development at higher elevations from Sunday through \nTuesday. However, limited shear and a lack of instability in the mid \nto upper levels should inhibit deep convection. Most if not all \nstorms are expected to remain in Canada rather than crossing the \nCoast Range into our AOR.\n\nThrough the weekend, a weak upper level trough is expected to move \nover the eastern Gulf, followed by the formation of a jet streak \nsouth of the region by Tuesday. This, combined with a low-pressure \ncenter tracking east from the Bering Sea across the Gulf, may \nprovide sufficient energy for widespread rain showers and higher QPF \namounts as a front pushes northward on Tuesday. While guidance is \nbeginning to agree on the track of the Gulf low, there is still \nlarge spread in rainfall totals, which will likely be more \ninfluenced by local effects than anything else.",
